.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt HWSTAT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hwstat
.Nd hardware telemetry with OpenTelemetry output
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm list
.Op Fl -format Ar text | json
.Op Fl -per-core
.Nm
.Cm watch
.Op Fl -interval Ar seconds
.Op Fl -duration Ar seconds
.Op Fl -per-core
.Op Fl -format Ar text | json | otel
.Op Fl -endpoint Ar url
.Nm
.Cm exec
.Op Fl -format Ar text | json
.Op Fl -per-core
.Fl -
.Ar command Op Ar args
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
collects CPU power consumption (Intel RAPL or AMD Zen package energy),
CPU temperatures,
frequencies, C-state usage, ACPI thermal zones, and GPU state,
and ships the results as text, JSONL, or OTLP/HTTP metrics to an
OpenTelemetry collector.
.Ss Subcommands
.Bl -tag -width "watch"
.It Cm list
Report CPU identity, RAPL power domains, temperatures, frequencies,
C-state support, ACPI thermal zones, and GPU state.
.It Cm watch
Sample telemetry at the given interval (default 1 second) until
interrupted or
.Fl -duration
elapses.
With
.Fl -per-core ,
CPU IDs are enumerated from the root CPU affinity set and may have gaps.
Interval accounting matches CPU IDs across snapshots, including changes
in the visible set.
Each logical CPU has a separate CPU ID, driver-reported frequency in MHz,
temperature, and C-state data when available.
Frequency readings are snapshots, not interval-average execution frequencies.
C-state percentages come from
.Va dev.cpu.N.cx_usage
and describe the distribution of accumulated idle-state entries, not
interval time residency.
C1, C2, and C3 are ACPI idle-state labels, generally offering greater power
savings and longer wake latency at deeper states.
The text headings use
.Dq entry%
to distinguish idle-entry distribution from elapsed-time percentages.
CPU utilization is computed from differences in
.Va kern.cp_times
between samples, with separate user, nice, system, interrupt, and idle
percentages in per-core JSON.
A reset, missing sample, or zero tick delta produces no utilization reading.
Scheduler idle accounting does not measure hardware C-state residency.
AMD may expose a shared package temperature through multiple logical CPUs;
these are not independent physical-core sensors.
With
.Fl -format Cm otel ,
each sample interval becomes a set of OTLP gauge metrics POSTed to
the collector's
.Pa /v1/metrics
endpoint.
.It Cm exec
Read RAPL energy counters and temperatures before and after running
the given command, then report total energy, average power, and
temperature delta.
The child's exit status is propagated.
.El
.Sh HARDWARE SUPPORT
Energy data requires a supported Intel CPU or AMD Zen family 17h, 19h,
or 1ah CPU with readable energy counters and read access to
.Pa /dev/cpuctl0 .
Device permissions normally require running as root for energy readings.
The device is opened read-only; no MSRs are written.
Energy domains are probed and unreadable domains are omitted.
AMD reports package energy only; Intel-only TDP and power-info registers
are not used on AMD.
Supported Silvermont Atom models use their microjoule and milliwatt unit
multipliers instead of the Core encoding.
Core-style TDP, minimum-power, and maximum-power fields are unavailable
on these Atom models.
Both watch and command measurement poll energy counters internally at most
one second apart, independently of the output interval.
Polling is shortened for finer counter units, using a conservative 10 kW
per-domain bound to avoid missing 32-bit counter wraps.
The wrap deadline is computed separately from the one-second polling cap,
using the shortest counter range among available domains.
A failed read or polling delay reaching that deadline invalidates the
measurement; no partial energy total is reported.
Suspend or long scheduling/export stalls can therefore terminate a watch.
Command measurement waits for child-exit notification between counter polls,
so short commands do not acquire an extra polling interval of energy.
Ordinary scheduling and counter-read latency still apply.
Command reports include
.Dq energy_status
.Pq available, unavailable, or read_error .
A measurement failure gives exit status 1 when the command itself succeeded;
a nonzero command exit status is preserved.
.Pp
Energy readings cover the package containing logical CPU 0, including
other workloads on that package, not the whole multi-socket machine or
only the child of
.Cm exec .
.Pp
The amd64 and i386 GENERIC kernels include
.Xr cpuctl 4 ,
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr amdsmn 4 ,
and
.Xr amdtemp 4 .
Intel and AMD temperature drivers attach only to matching hardware.
On older or custom kernels, authorized sessions can use
.Xr sysextctl 8
to load the relevant drivers.
Raspberry Pi uses the Broadcom platform driver rather than the x86 drivers.
The arm64 GENERIC configuration includes the BCM2837/BCM2838 driver path
used for Pi 3/4.
The driver exposes SoC temperature through
.Va dev.cpu.0.temperature
and CPU frequency through the cpufreq interface, which
.Nm
reads without a Pi-specific backend.
These are shared SoC readings, not independent sensors for every core.
The Broadcom firmware backend also reads ARM, core, and SDRAM clocks,
SoC temperature, and
.Va hw.cpufreq.throttled .
Current undervoltage, frequency-cap, throttle, and soft-temperature-limit
flags are separate from historical flags.
Reading the new throttle sysctl does not clear firmware history.
There is no Pi energy-counter or voltage backend;
this driver path does not establish Pi 5 support.
.Pp
The JSON
.Dq temperatures
array preserves the sysctl source name and Celsius reading for each
available AMD sensor and ACPI thermal zone.
It is included by
.Cm list
and
.Cm watch ,
including per-core output.
ACPI thresholds and unreadable sensors are omitted.
OTLP exports these as thermal temperature_millicelsius gauges with a
source attribute.
.Nm
degrades gracefully when energy access or a sensor is unavailable:
the sysctl-based
sensors (temperatures, frequencies, C-states via
.Xr acpi 4 ,
thermal zones) are architecture-independent and work on arm64 and
riscv given the appropriate platform drivers, while all power
columns are simply omitted.
.Ss CPU capabilities and clock limits
.Cm list
also reports the boot-time
.Va hw.clockrate ,
the minimum and maximum entries in each exported cpufreq frequency table,
and AMD CPPC capability and request values, Intel HWP capabilities and
energy preference, and Pi firmware clock limits when available.
A one-entry cpufreq table does not establish the hardware's minimum or
maximum clock, especially with autonomous frequency selection.
The boot clock is not an advertised boost limit.
AMD CPPC highest, nominal, efficient, and lowest capabilities and requested
performance limits use abstract performance units, not MHz.
The read-only
.Va dev.hwpstate_amd.N.caps_*
capability sysctls require the updated driver.
Intel
.Va dev.hwpstate_intel.N.caps_*
sysctls expose highest, guaranteed, efficient, and lowest HWP capabilities.
These also use abstract performance units; guaranteed and efficient values
can change at runtime and are read afresh.
Intel EPP is reported as a 0-100 percentage, while AMD EPP uses 0-255 units.
Where CPUID leaf 16H supplies nonzero values, the Intel driver also exposes
base, maximum, and bus/reference specification frequencies in MHz.
These are informational specifications, not measured clocks or guaranteed
sustained/per-core turbo limits; unsupported fields remain absent.
Pi firmware ARM, core, and SDRAM minimum/maximum clocks are exported under
.Va hw.cpufreq
as read-only
.Va *_min_freq
and
.Va *_max_freq
sysctls in Hz.
They describe firmware configuration, not immutable product specifications.
.Nm
never changes performance requests or idle-state policy.
.Ss Additional driver metrics
AMD GPU metrics tables are read from
.Va sys.device.drmnN.gpu_metrics
for device units 0 through 63.
The binary transport requires a rebuilt DRM module using the updated
LinuxKPI headers as well as a compatible kernel.
A kernel rebuild alone does not update an already built or loaded DRM module.
Only complete version 2.1 and 2.2 tables are decoded.
Supported fields include graphics and SoC temperatures, graphics and media
activity, socket and component power, clocks, and physical-core sensor slots.
Unpopulated firmware fields are omitted.
These firmware core indices are not logical CPU IDs and are not mapped to
SMT siblings.
Socket power overlaps CPU package energy and must not be added to it.
The raw throttle bitmap is ASIC-dependent; it is not decoded into universal
AMD limit reasons.
.Pp
Intel
.Xr coretemp 4
also exposes a non-clearing thermal status reading.
With the updated driver, CPU temperature is derived from that same status
snapshot and the cached Tjmax value, without reading the legacy temperature
or throttle-log handlers that clear hardware log bits.
On older kernels those legacy handlers remain the fallback.
Other consumers can still clear hardware logs.
Current thermal throttling is separate from the historical throttle log.
This is not a model-specific power-limit-reason decoder.
.Pp
JSON includes
.Dq metrics
entries with source, name, and integer value.
Names include units: milliwatts, millicelsius, MHz or Hz, and basis points
.Pq 100 basis points equal one percent .
.Dq metric_sources
reports whether each discovered AMD table is available, has an invalid
length, uses an unsupported revision, or could not be read.
An absent source or field is not a zero measurement.
These metrics are included in text watch and command output, OTLP watch
output, and under
.Dq after
in JSON command reports.
Firmware averages use firmware-defined windows, not the watch interval.
Watch intervals range from 0.001 through 3600 seconds.
A duration shorter than the interval produces a final partial sample
at the duration deadline.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The OTLP exporter honors
.Ev OTEL_SERVICE_NAME ,
.Ev OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS ,
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION ,
and
.Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Sample every 2 seconds with per-core detail:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hwstat watch --per-core --interval 2
.Ed
.Pp
Ship metrics to an OpenTelemetry collector for 5 minutes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hwstat watch --format otel --endpoint http://collector:4318 \e
    --duration 300
.Ed
.Pp
Measure the energy cost of a build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hwstat exec -- make -j20
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr amdsmn 4 ,
.Xr amdtemp 4 ,
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr cpuctl 4 ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8 ,
.Xr sysextctl 8
